-- title:
--   Weyl: the cone generated by finitely many vectors of ℝ^m is closed
-- statement:
--   **Weyl's theorem on finitely generated cones.** Let $v_1,\dots,v_n \in \mathbb{R}^m$. The cone they generate,
--
--   $$\operatorname{cone}(v_1,\dots,v_n) \;=\; \Big\{\, \sum_{j=1}^{n} y_j v_j \;:\; y_1,\dots,y_n \ge 0 \,\Big\},$$
--
--   is a closed subset of $\mathbb{R}^m$.
--
--   Closedness is not automatic: the cone is the image of the closed but unbounded set $\mathbb{R}^n_{\ge 0}$ under a linear map, and linear images of closed sets need not be closed. The result is one half of the Minkowski-Weyl theorem (a V-cone is an H-cone), and it is the topological input to Farkas' lemma, to linear programming duality, and to the fact that the feasible set of a stochastic program with fixed recourse is closed.
--
--   **Formalization note.** Vectors are functions out of `Fin m`, carrying the product topology, which for a finite index set is the usual Euclidean topology. The generators are given as a family `v : Fin n → (Fin m → ℝ)`; taking `v j` to be the $j$-th column of a matrix $A$ gives the closedness of $\{A y : y \ge 0\}$.
-- source:
--   H. Weyl, Elementare Theorie der konvexen Polyeder, Comment. Math. Helv. 7 (1935), 290-306; see also D. Bertsimas and J. N. Tsitsiklis, Introduction to Linear Optimization, Athena Scientific 1997, Section 4.7

import Mathlib

theorem Polyhedral.isClosed_conicSpan {m n : ℕ} (v : Fin n → (Fin m → ℝ)) :
    IsClosed {z : Fin m → ℝ | ∃ y : Fin n → ℝ, (∀ j, 0 ≤ y j) ∧ ∑ j, y j • v j = z} := by sorry
